# Cultural Landscapes Inventory: First Day - Union 1st Corps Battlefield (National Park Service, 2021)
# Read: PDF pp. 35-38, 43 (physical history to 1863 and aftermath), 44-52 (later periods, digest),
#       54-62 (natural systems, spatial organization: field by field), 76-81 (vegetation, buildings and structures).
# The inventory says its text is extracted from the 2021 Cultural Landscape Report for the same area (NPS-CLR-1STDAY).
@ NPS-CLI-1STDAY

# --- 1863 landscape ---
37 | general | Landscape northwest of Gettysburg, July 1863 | bld-mcpherson-farm,bld-herbst | 1863-07 | stated | The First Day ground was an orthogonal pattern of fenced fields with widely scattered woodlots and three main roads; Willoughby Run had rocky banks lined by woods, willow clumps and briars | Through the center of this rolling landscape was Willoughby Run, >>> and Hagerstown Road (Figure 4).
37 | fields | Farms of the First Day field, general | - | 1863-07 | stated | The farms had fenced fields, orchards, woodlots and a farmstead of house, barns and outbuildings; town outlots of two to thirteen acres served as woodlots or pasture | These outlots, between two and thirteen acres in size, >>> barns, and other outbuildings.
37 | earthworks | Seminary Grove rail breastworks, July 1 | E-01,FEN-seminary-ridge,bld-schmucker-hall | 1863-07-01 | stated | Buford used the Seminary cupola as a signal station from June 30 while his cavalry division camped at the McPherson farm; Union troops later assembled stacked-rail breastworks along the west edge of Seminary Grove | Arriving on June 30, General Buford used the cupola of the Seminary building >>> west edge of Seminary Grove.
38 | earthworks | Confederate breastworks on Seminary Ridge and Oak Ridge, July 4 | E-18 | 1863-07-04 | stated | On July 4 Confederate troops built and strengthened breastworks along the whole ridge from Oak Hill south to the Emmitsburg Road: stacked rails, possibly reinforced with earth and stone, through the seminary campus; earthen breastworks dug by Daniel's and O'Neal's brigades in the woods of Oak Ridge north of the railroad cut | he ordered his troops to spend the following day, July 4, >>> dug earthen breastworks through the woods.
38 | burials | Burial concentrations on the First Day field | bld-mcpherson-farm,bld-herbst | 1863-07 (after the battle) | stated | Burials were concentrated in Seminary Grove, in Herbst Woods and along Willoughby Run, near the railroad cuts on McPherson's Ridge, at the McPherson and Forney farmsteads, west of the Thompson house, on the Wills farm and on the eastern part of the Forney farm | Concentrations of burials were in Seminary Grove, >>> eastern part of the Forney Farm near Oak Ridge.
43 | orchards | Dustman orchard on Seminary Ridge at the Chambersburg Pike | bld-thompson-house | 1863 | stated | An 1863 view from the later Lee's Headquarters site shows an orchard that belonged to C. H. Dustman in the foreground | The orchard in the foreground belonged to C.H. Dustman.
43 | burials | Reinterments October 1863 to March 1864 | WS-081-reburials | !1863-10 to 1864-03 (reinterments after the battle) | stated | Between October 1863 and March 1864, 3,512 Union remains were moved from battlefield graves to the Soldiers' National Cemetery; 1,664 could not be identified by name or state | Between October 1863 and March 1864, 3,512 Union remains >>> could not be identified by name or state.
44 | buildings | Repairs after the battle | - | ~1863-1895 (after the battle) | stated | The seminary and most farms of the First Day field were repaired soon after the battle, with some changes to buildings, orchards and fence lines | The seminary and most of the farms on the 1st Corps battlefield were repaired soon after the battle ended, with some changes and improvements to buildings, orchards, and fence lines.
45 | avenues | Buford and Robinson avenues | RD-avenues | !1889 and shortly before 1895 | stated | Buford Avenue was added in 1889 and Robinson Avenue shortly before 1895 | Buford Avenue was added in 1889, and Robinson Avenue shortly before 1895.
49 | fences | Park treatment of walls and fences after 1895 | FEN-cemetery-ridge-walls | ~1895-1933 treatment | stated | The War Department park preserved and re-established woodlots, fields, stone walls and fences, sometimes at pristine conditions that may not have been characteristic of the landscape in 1863 | The park’s treatment of federally-owned 1863 battlefield landscape >>> characteristic of the landscape in 1863.
49 | fences | Fences on private farms, 1895-1933 | - | ~1895-1933 | stated | On farms outside park ownership rail and Virginia worm fences were still common between 1895 and 1933 but were replaced in many areas by wire fences | Most of the battlefield farms outside of park ownership retained their battle-era field patterns, with rail and Virginia worm fences still common, but replaced in many areas with more economical wire fences.
50 | fences | Fence lines rebuilt and orchards replanted from the early 2000s | - | 2000s reconstruction (modern) | stated | By the early 2000s the park was clearing woods on battle-era fields, replanting orchards and rebuilding fence lines | By the early 2000s, the park and its partners were acquiring land, removing non-historic buildings, clearing woods on battle-era fields, replanting orchards, and rebuilding fence lines.

# --- Natural features and woods ---
54 | woodlots | Willoughby Run riparian woods | - | 1863-07; more than half grown since the early 20th century | stated | Riparian woods existed along Willoughby Run during the battle, but more than half of the present woods have grown up since the early 20th century | Riparian woods existed during the battle along Willoughby Run, but more than half of the existing woods have grown up since the early 20th century,

