# --- Vegetation ---
76 | orchards | Dustman (Lee's Headquarters) orchard | bld-thompson-house | replanting (modern, date not given on the page) | stated | The replanted Lee's Headquarters (Dustman) orchard is immature and missing over half its trees; the eastern part of the battle-era orchard is now residential grounds | The replanted Lee’s Headquarters’ Orchard, although still immature and missing over half of the trees, reestablishes historic vegetation in the battlefield landscape.
77 | woodlots | Herbst Woods | bld-herbst | 1863-07; later change | stated | Herbst Woods has stayed largely intact since the battle except for some expansion of the wooded area and the loss of its open understory | Herbst Woods is key terrain on the 1st Corps battlefield that has remained largely intact since the battle, >>> and the loss of open understory.
77 | woodlots | McPherson Woods | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07; later change | stated | McPherson Woods keeps its overall battle character and limits but has grown denser, lost its eastern and southern parts to the railroad and expanded into McPherson Field 10 | McPherson Woods retains its overall battle character and limits. >>> expansion of the woods into McPherson Field #10.
77 | orchards | McPherson fruit garden | bld-mcpherson-farm | replanting (modern) | stated | The replanted McPherson fruit garden is immature; the adjacent farmhouse, fences, lane and barnyard are lost | The McPherson fruit garden is immature and has not yet reestablished its historic spatial character.
78 | woodlots | Forney Woods | - | ~modern 2021 extent beyond the 1863 woodlot | stated | Forney Woods has re-established itself but now extends beyond the limits of the 1863 woodlot | The Forney woods has become reestablished but has expanded beyond the limits of the 1863 woodlot,
78 | orchards | Gilbert orchard | - | replanting (modern) larger than in 1863 | stated | The replanted Gilbert orchard is larger than the orchard that existed in 1863 | but the extent of the orchard is greater than what existed in 1863.
78 | orchards | Forney and McClean orchards | - | replanting (modern) | stated | The replanted Forney and McClean orchards have different varieties and tree spacing from the battle-era orchards | will reestablish the overall character and limits of the battle-era orchard, but with different varieties and tree spacing. ~#1
79 | woodlots | Harman and Wible woodlots (Herr's Ridge, Springs Hotel Woods) | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The Harman and Wible woodlots keep their battle-era limits and native oak-hickory community inside the park; in 1863 they were managed woodlots that most likely had a more open understory | The Harman-Wible Woodlots retain battle-era limits and native oak-hickory community >>> more open understory.
79 | woodlots | Evergreens on the First Day field | - | 1863-07 | stated | Evergreens were not part of the battlefield landscape | Evergreens were not part of the battlefield landscape. ~#1

# --- Buildings and structures ---
80 | fences | Seminary Avenue stone wall (Rodes's Division breastworks) | E-18 | ~1895-1912 commemorative-era construction | hedged | The stone wall breastworks along Seminary Ridge avenue belong to the commemorative landscape built by the park commission and may originally have had a more refined construction, like other walls the commission rebuilt | The stone wall breastworks along Seminary Ridge (avenue) are part of the commemorative landscape developed under the park commission. >>> GNPC-rebuilt stone walls on the battlefield.
80 | buildings | McPherson barn | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 (restored exterior) | stated | The McPherson barn is the only battle-era building on park property within the First Corps battlefield; it has been restored to its battle-era exterior appearance and its farmstead setting is lost | The McPherson Barn has been restored to its battle-era exterior appearance and is the only battleera building on park property within the 1st Corps battlefield.
81 | buildings | Wills (Bender) farmhouse, carriage house and storage house | - | !post-battle buildings | stated | The Bender (Wills-Weinbrenner) farmhouse, carriage house and storage house on the Wills farm are not battle-period buildings | Although not a battle-period building, the Bender House reflects construction of the period and maintains the farmstead cluster on the battle-era Wills Farm landscape
81 | buildings | Wills barn | - | 1863-07; later paint and additions | stated | The Wills barn is one of only two battle-era park-owned buildings on the First Corps battlefield; its white paint and additions post-date the battle | The Wills Barn, one of only two battle-era park-owned buildings on the 1st Corps battlefield, retains its overall character. The existing white paint and additions post-date the battle.
81 | fences | Doubleday and North Confederate Avenue stone walls | - | ~modern 2021 condition | hedged | The stone walls along Doubleday and North Confederate avenues now have a tumbled-down character that may have existed during the battle | The Doubleday and North Confederate Avenue stone walls presently reflect a tumbled-down character that may have existed during the battle.
81 | monuments | Eternal Light Peace Memorial | - | !1938 | stated | The Eternal Light Peace Memorial (designed by Paul Cret) is a later feature of Oak Hill | The Peace Memorial retains its historic character as designed by Paul Cret,
81 | monuments | Oak Ridge observation tower | - | !park commission era (after 1895) | stated | The Oak Ridge observation tower, now shortened to a platform, was built by the park commission | The Oak Ridge observation tower, now shortened to an elevated platform, is an important component of the commemorative park landscape developed by the GNPC.
